window.BOOKDATA={"SRC": {"1": {"t": "World Sailing, The Racing Rules of Sailing for 2025-2028, with changes and corrections to April 2026. Rule 10: when boats are on opposite tacks, a port-tack boat keeps clear of a starboard-tack boat.", "u": ["https://www.sailing.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/2025-2028-RRS-with-Changes-and-Corrections.pdf"]}, "2": {"t": "World Sailing, The Racing Rules of Sailing for 2025-2028, with changes and corrections to April 2026. Rule 11: when boats are on the same tack and overlapped, a windward boat keeps clear of a leeward boat. Preamble to Part 2 Section C: the mark rules do not apply at a starting mark surrounded by navigable water, from the time the boats are approaching it to start until they have left it astern.", "u": ["https://www.sailing.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/2025-2028-RRS-with-Changes-and-Corrections.pdf"]}, "3": {"t": "World Sailing, The Racing Rules of Sailing for 2025-2028, with changes and corrections to April 2026. Rule 26, Starting races: the warning signal five minutes before the starting signal, the preparatory signal four minutes before, the one-minute signal, then the starting signal.", "u": ["https://www.sailing.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/2025-2028-RRS-with-Changes-and-Corrections.pdf"]}, "4": {"t": "World Sailing, The Racing Rules of Sailing for 2025-2028, with changes and corrections to April 2026. Rule 29, Recalls: flag X with one sound for an individual recall, the First Substitute with two sounds for a general recall; rule 29.1 does not apply when the U flag or the black flag applies. Rule 30, Starting penalties: the I flag, the Z flag, the U flag and the black flag, each applying in the last minute before the starting signal. Z: the scoring penalty. U: disqualified without a hearing, unless the race is restarted. Black: disqualified without a hearing even if the race is restarted, with the sail number displayed before the next warning signal. Rule 21.1 (rule 22.1 in the previous book): a boat sailing back to the pre-start side after the starting signal keeps clear of boats that are not. The definition Start: the hull entirely on the pre-start side, then crossing the line. Rule 44.3(c): when the notice of race or sailing instructions state no number of points, the scoring penalty is twenty per cent of the score for Did Not Finish, rounded to the nearest tenth of a point, and it cannot make a boat's score worse than that score. Appendix A: under the low point system first place scores one point, second two, and so on; a boat that did not finish, or was over the line and did not return, scores one more than the number of boats entered in the series. Summarised here as in the coaching manuals the book is built from; the rule text decides.", "u": ["https://www.sailing.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/2025-2028-RRS-with-Changes-and-Corrections.pdf"]}, "5": {"t": "World Sailing, The Racing Rules of Sailing for 2025-2028, with changes and corrections to April 2026. Rule 13: after a boat passes head to wind she keeps clear of other boats until she is on a close-hauled course. Rule 15: a boat that acquires right of way must at first give the other boat room to keep clear, unless she acquires it because of the other boat's actions. Rule 16.2: on a beat to windward, when a port-tack boat is keeping clear by sailing to pass to leeward of a starboard-tack boat, the starboard-tack boat does not bear away if the port-tack boat would then have to change course at once to keep clear. Summarised here as in the coaching manuals the book is built from; the rule text decides.", "u": ["https://www.sailing.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/2025-2028-RRS-with-Changes-and-Corrections.pdf"]}, "6": {"t": "World Sailing, The Racing Rules of Sailing for 2025-2028, with changes and corrections to April 2026. Definitions: the zone is the area within three hull lengths of a mark; a boat is fetching a mark when she can pass to windward of it and leave it on the required side without changing tack; mark-room is room for a boat to leave a mark on the required side, to sail to the mark when her proper course is to sail close to it, and to round the mark as needed to sail the course; boats are overlapped when neither is clear astern of the other. Rule 18.1: the mark rules apply between boats required to leave a mark on the same side when at least one of them is in the zone, but not between boats on opposite tacks on a beat to windward. Rule 18.2: when boats are overlapped the outside boat gives the inside boat mark-room, and if they are overlapped when the first of them reaches the zone the outside boat at that moment gives mark-room from then on; a boat clear ahead when she reaches the zone is given mark-room by the boat clear astern, and that continues even if the overlap is later broken or a new one begins. Rule 18.3: when a boat passes head to wind from port to starboard in the zone of a mark to be left to port, rule 18.2 does not apply between her and a boat on starboard that is fetching the mark; if that boat has been on starboard since entering the zone, the boat that tacked must not make her sail above close-hauled to avoid contact, and gives her mark-room if she becomes overlapped inside. Summarised here as in the coaching manuals the book is built from; the rule text decides.", "u": ["https://www.sailing.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/2025-2028-RRS-with-Changes-and-Corrections.pdf"]}, "7": {"t": "World Sailing, The Racing Rules of Sailing for 2025-2028, with changes and corrections to April 2026. Rule 1.1: a boat or competitor gives all possible help to any person or vessel in danger. Rule 3: the decision to race, or to keep racing, is the boat's alone. Rule 41(a): a boat may be helped when a crew member is ill, injured or in danger. Rule 61.4(b)(4): a boat whose score was made significantly worse because she gave that help may ask for redress. Rule 21.3: a boat moving backwards, or sideways to windward, by backing her sail keeps clear of any boat that is not. Definition keep clear: a boat keeps clear of a right-of-way boat if the right-of-way boat can sail her course with no need to take avoiding action and, when the boats are overlapped, the right-of-way boat can also change course in both directions without immediately making contact. Rule 12: when boats are on the same tack and not overlapped, the boat clear astern keeps clear of the boat clear ahead. Rule 13, second part: when two boats are tacking at the same time, the one on the other's port side, or the one astern, keeps clear. Rule 14: a boat shall avoid contact with another boat if reasonably possible; a right-of-way boat need not act to avoid contact until it is clear that the other boat is not keeping clear, and under rule 43.1(c) she is exonerated for breaking rule 14 unless the contact causes damage or injury. Rule 17: a boat that becomes overlapped to leeward from clear astern, within two of her hull lengths of the other boat on the same tack, shall not sail above her proper course while they remain overlapped and within that distance, unless she promptly sails astern of the other boat; the rule applies only to a boat that became overlapped from clear astern, so not to one that completes a tack to leeward of the other boat; before the starting signal a boat has no proper course, so the limit does not apply then. Rule 16.1: when a right-of-way boat changes course she gives the other boat room to keep clear. Rule 31: while racing a boat shall not touch a mark. Rule 44.1 and 44.2: a boat that may have broken a rule of Part 2 in an incident while racing may take a Two-Turns Penalty, and a boat that may have broken rule 31 a One-Turn Penalty; she gets well clear of other boats as soon after the incident as possible and promptly makes the turns in the same direction, each turn including one tack and one gybe; under rule 44.1(b), if she caused injury or serious damage, or gained a significant advantage despite the turns, her penalty is to retire. Rule 60.2(a)(1): a boat that intends to protest about an incident hails Protest at the first reasonable opportunity. Summarised here as in the coaching manuals the book is built from; the rule text decides.", "u": ["https://www.sailing.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/2025-2028-RRS-with-Changes-and-Corrections.pdf"]}}, "WSRC": {}, "BUILD": "build 31, 1 November 2026", "BOOK": [{"key": "1", "num": "1", "title": "The Look", "part": "Part I — Read the wind", "sections": [{"num": "1.1", "title": "The wind is patchy"}, {"num": "1.2", "title": "Count to 20 and look up the course"}, {"num": "1.3", "title": "More wind usually wins"}, {"num": "1.4", "title": "Clues that can fool you"}, {"num": "1.5", "title": "Gust lanes and the shore"}]}, {"key": "2", "num": "2", "title": "Lift or header?", "part": "Part I — Read the wind", "sections": [{"num": "2.1", "title": "What a wind shift is"}, {"num": "2.2", "title": "Watch your bow against the mark"}, {"num": "2.3", "title": "Let the other boats tell you"}, {"num": "2.4", "title": "Real shift or just a wobble?"}, {"num": "2.5", "title": "Find your normal on each tack"}]}, {"key": "3", "num": "3", "title": "Ladder rungs: why the lifted tack wins", "part": "Part I — Read the wind", "sections": [{"num": "3.1", "title": "What the rungs are"}, {"num": "3.2", "title": "A shift turns the ladder"}, {"num": "3.3", "title": "Tack on the header"}, {"num": "3.4", "title": "The further apart, the bigger the jump"}, {"num": "3.5", "title": "Never go to the corner"}]}, {"key": "4", "num": "4", "title": "Which shift today?", "part": "Part I — Read the wind", "sections": [{"num": "4.1", "title": "Swinging shifts: tack on the headers"}, {"num": "4.2", "title": "One-way shifts: go towards the shift"}, {"num": "4.3", "title": "No clear pattern"}, {"num": "4.4", "title": "Find out before the start"}, {"num": "4.5", "title": "The embat at your club"}, {"num": "4.6", "title": "More wind or a better angle?"}]}, {"key": "5", "num": "5", "title": "Current and waves", "part": "Part I — Read the wind", "sections": [{"num": "5.1", "title": "How current moves the course"}, {"num": "5.2", "title": "Test the current"}, {"num": "5.3", "title": "Current and the start line"}, {"num": "5.4", "title": "Waves: pick your tack and your moment"}]}, {"key": "6", "num": "6", "title": "Make good decisions", "part": "Part II — Make a plan", "sections": [{"num": "6.1", "title": "Reach the first mark in the leading group"}, {"num": "6.2", "title": "Strategy and tactics"}, {"num": "6.3", "title": "Fast, clear, correct"}, {"num": "6.4", "title": "Five things to read"}, {"num": "6.5", "title": "What comes first"}, {"num": "6.6", "title": "Look, choose, sail, look again"}]}, {"key": "7", "num": "7", "title": "Read the race area", "part": "Part II — Make a plan", "sections": [{"num": "7.1", "title": "Arrive early"}, {"num": "7.2", "title": "Sail both sides"}, {"num": "7.3", "title": "The split test with a partner"}, {"num": "7.4", "title": "The mark, the skew and the long tack"}, {"num": "7.5", "title": "Watch the fleet"}]}, {"key": "8", "num": "8", "title": "Choose your side", "part": "Part II — Make a plan", "sections": [{"num": "8.1", "title": "When the left pays"}, {"num": "8.2", "title": "When the right pays"}, {"num": "8.3", "title": "When the middle is best"}, {"num": "8.4", "title": "How sure are you?"}, {"num": "8.5", "title": "Your one-sentence plan"}, {"num": "8.6", "title": "Plan A and the trigger for Plan B"}]}, {"key": "9", "num": "9", "title": "Which end, which side?", "part": "Part III — The start", "sections": [{"num": "9.1", "title": "The start is the first move of the beat"}, {"num": "9.2", "title": "Which end? The head-to-wind test"}, {"num": "9.3", "title": "Favoured end or best place to start?"}, {"num": "9.4", "title": "Start where your side is"}, {"num": "9.5", "title": "A gap beats the perfect end"}, {"num": "9.6", "title": "Your start card"}]}, {"key": "10", "num": "10", "title": "Big-fleet starts", "part": "Part III — The start", "sections": [{"num": "10.1", "title": "Where is the line? The middle sags"}, {"num": "10.2", "title": "Your transit"}, {"num": "10.3", "title": "Where will the crowd go?"}, {"num": "10.4", "title": "Stay out of the piles"}, {"num": "10.5", "title": "Choose your lane early"}, {"num": "10.6", "title": "Your first move after the gun"}]}, {"key": "11", "num": "11", "title": "The last five minutes", "part": "Part III — The start", "sections": [{"num": "11.1", "title": "A routine for five minutes"}, {"num": "11.2", "title": "Check again: wind, pressure and end"}, {"num": "11.3", "title": "Gaps open late"}, {"num": "11.4", "title": "Your final approach"}, {"num": "11.5", "title": "Make a gap and keep it"}, {"num": "11.6", "title": "Time, distance, speed, space: go at 10 seconds"}, {"num": "11.7", "title": "Know your escape"}, {"num": "11.8", "title": "The ten-second question"}, {"num": "11.9", "title": "At the gun"}]}, {"key": "12", "num": "12", "title": "Know the starting flags", "part": "Part III — The start", "sections": [{"num": "12.1", "title": "P: the normal start"}, {"num": "12.2", "title": "I: round an end"}, {"num": "12.3", "title": "Z: a scoring penalty"}, {"num": "12.4", "title": "U and black: out of the race"}, {"num": "12.5", "title": "Rules around the starting marks"}]}, {"key": "13", "num": "13", "title": "The first minute", "part": "Part IV — The first beat", "sections": [{"num": "13.1", "title": "Speed first"}, {"num": "13.2", "title": "Did the start give you your lane?"}, {"num": "13.3", "title": "When your lane is dying"}, {"num": "13.4", "title": "Going right: your first escape"}, {"num": "13.5", "title": "Going left: keep your lane"}, {"num": "13.6", "title": "Duck a few boats for a better lane"}, {"num": "13.7", "title": "No panic tacks"}, {"num": "13.8", "title": "Back to your plan"}]}, {"key": "14", "num": "14", "title": "The beat in three parts", "part": "Part IV — The first beat", "sections": [{"num": "14.1", "title": "Lower third: fast and free"}, {"num": "14.2", "title": "Middle third: compare on the ladder"}, {"num": "14.3", "title": "Upper third: bank your gains"}, {"num": "14.4", "title": "When the picture is unclear: the long tack"}, {"num": "14.5", "title": "Check your plan after every big change"}]}, {"key": "15", "num": "15", "title": "Race the boats around you", "part": "Part IV — The first beat", "sections": [{"num": "15.1", "title": "Clear air is worth a lot"}, {"num": "15.2", "title": "Stay with the leaders, but think"}, {"num": "15.3", "title": "Bank a gain"}, {"num": "15.4", "title": "Don't be the outside boat"}, {"num": "15.5", "title": "How much risk? It depends on the race"}, {"num": "15.6", "title": "Loose cover or your own race"}, {"num": "15.7", "title": "Protect or attack"}, {"num": "15.8", "title": "What can hurt me next?"}]}, {"key": "16", "num": "16", "title": "Cross, tack or duck", "part": "Part IV — The first beat", "sections": [{"num": "16.1", "title": "On starboard: hold, lee-bow or wave through"}, {"num": "16.2", "title": "On port: cross, tack or duck"}, {"num": "16.3", "title": "Pick the smallest loss"}, {"num": "16.4", "title": "Tack in front or behind a group"}, {"num": "16.5", "title": "Protect your lane without going the wrong way"}, {"num": "16.6", "title": "Escape without losing twice"}, {"num": "16.7", "title": "Race the boat or race the course?"}, {"num": "16.8", "title": "A good choice can still lose"}]}, {"key": "17", "num": "17", "title": "Laylines and the windward mark", "part": "Part IV — The first beat", "sections": [{"num": "17.1", "title": "See both laylines"}, {"num": "17.2", "title": "Why early laylines cost"}, {"num": "17.3", "title": "The starboard layline queue"}, {"num": "17.4", "title": "Come in from below the layline"}, {"num": "17.5", "title": "The port approach"}, {"num": "17.6", "title": "Your last tack"}, {"num": "17.7", "title": "The zone and mark-room"}, {"num": "17.8", "title": "Plan the exit"}, {"num": "17.9", "title": "Judge the beat by your choices"}]}, {"key": "18", "num": "18", "title": "Rules that shape your choices", "part": "Part IV — The first beat", "sections": [{"num": "18.1", "title": "Port and starboard"}, {"num": "18.2", "title": "Windward, leeward, ahead and astern"}, {"num": "18.3", "title": "While you tack"}, {"num": "18.4", "title": "Avoid contact, and give room when you change course"}, {"num": "18.5", "title": "Your rules check"}]}, {"key": "19", "num": "19", "title": "Your speed platform", "part": "Part V — Speed toolkit", "sections": [{"num": "19.1", "title": "Strategy needs a fast boat"}, {"num": "19.2", "title": "Check before you launch"}, {"num": "19.3", "title": "Settings for light, medium and strong wind"}, {"num": "19.4", "title": "Telltales and your gears"}]}, {"key": "20", "num": "20", "title": "Gusts, lulls and strong wind", "part": "Part V — Speed toolkit", "sections": [{"num": "20.1", "title": "See the gust before it hits"}, {"num": "20.2", "title": "Learn the gust in five stages"}, {"num": "20.3", "title": "Lulls"}, {"num": "20.4", "title": "When it is really windy"}]}, {"key": "21", "num": "21", "title": "The race card", "part": "Part VI — Every race", "sections": [{"num": "21.1", "title": "Before the start: tap what you see"}, {"num": "21.2", "title": "One first move"}, {"num": "21.3", "title": "After the race: one honest reason"}, {"num": "21.4", "title": "Draw the first beat"}, {"num": "21.5", "title": "Pocket cards"}]}, {"key": "22", "num": "22", "title": "Practise on the water", "part": "Part VI — Every race", "sections": [{"num": "22.1", "title": "How to practise"}, {"num": "22.2", "title": "Sessions for reading the course"}, {"num": "22.3", "title": "Sessions for the start"}, {"num": "22.4", "title": "Sessions for the beat"}, {"num": "22.5", "title": "A session for gusts"}]}, {"key": "23", "num": "23", "title": "Learn from every race", "part": "Part VI — Every race", "sections": [{"num": "23.1", "title": "Five questions after every race"}, {"num": "23.2", "title": "Your scorecard"}, {"num": "23.3", "title": "Track how you race, not only where you finish"}, {"num": "23.4", "title": "Judge the decision, not the result"}]}], "GLOSS": {"G1": {"t": "Ladder rung", "b": "A rung is an imaginary line across the course, at right angles to the wind. The mark sits at the top of the ladder.\nBoats on the same rung are level: they have the same amount of upwind still to sail, however far apart they are, as long as neither has sailed past a layline. When the wind changes direction the rungs turn with it, and that is what moves boats ahead or behind."}, "G2": {"t": "Wind shift", "b": "A change in the direction the wind comes from. A shift to the right means the wind now comes from further right as you look up the course towards the mark.\nAt the club: rolada. “El viento rola” means the wind is shifting."}, "G3": {"t": "Lift", "b": "A shift that lets you point higher, closer to straight upwind, on the tack you are on. In most of the course you see it as your bow swinging towards the mark. Keep going.\nThe same shift is a header for the boats on the other tack."}, "G4": {"t": "Header", "b": "A shift that pushes your bow lower, further from straight upwind, on the tack you are on. In most of the course you see it as your bow swinging away from the mark. On a day when the wind swings back and forth, a real header means tack.\nThe same shift is a lift for the boats on the other tack."}, "G5": {"t": "Lifted tack", "b": "The tack that points closer to straight upwind right now. When the wind is to the right of its middle direction, it is starboard tack. When the wind is to the left of it, it is port tack. When your bow is lower than normal on one tack, the other tack is the lifted one."}, "G6": {"t": "Tack", "b": "To turn the bow through the wind, from port tack to starboard tack or back again.\nAt the club: virar."}, "G7": {"t": "Port tack and starboard tack", "b": "You are on starboard tack when the wind comes over the right-hand side of your boat and your boom is out on the left. On port tack it is the other way round.\nWhen two boats on opposite tacks meet, the boat on port must keep clear of the boat on starboard.\nAt the club: amurado a babor, amurado a estribor."}, "G8": {"t": "Layline", "b": "The line from which you can just reach the mark on one tack, sailing close-hauled. When you reach it, you tack once and sail straight to the mark."}, "G9": {"t": "Overstand", "b": "To sail past the layline, so that after your last tack you have to ease the sheet and reach down to the mark. The extra distance is wasted."}, "G10": {"t": "Leverage", "b": "How far apart you are sideways from another boat or group. More leverage means a bigger gain if the next shift comes your way, and a bigger loss if it goes the other way."}, "G11": {"t": "Close-hauled", "b": "Sailing as close to the wind as you can while the sail still drives the boat. On the beat you sail close-hauled on port and starboard in turn.\nAt the club: ceñir."}, "G12": {"t": "The mark", "b": "In this book, the mark always means the windward mark at the top of the first beat. In the pictures it sits right under the wind arrow.\nAt the club: boya, or boya de barlovento. The rule book calls it a baliza."}, "G13": {"t": "Beat", "b": "The upwind leg. You sail it in a zigzag because no boat can sail straight into the wind.\nAt the club: ceñida."}, "G14": {"t": "Swinging shift", "b": "Wind that swings left, then right, then left again, around its average direction. Tack on the headers. Coaches also call it an oscillating shift.\nAt the club: rolada oscilante."}, "G15": {"t": "One-way shift", "b": "Wind that keeps turning the same way. The side it is turning towards wins, so go there first, even on a header. Coaches also call it a persistent shift.\nAt the club: rolada persistente."}, "G16": {"t": "Gust", "b": "A patch of stronger wind. It usually shows as darker, rougher water moving down the course.\nAt the club: racha."}, "G17": {"t": "Embat", "b": "The afternoon sea breeze in Mallorca. It fills in from the sea as the land heats up during the day.\nAt the club: embat."}, "G18": {"t": "Dirty air", "b": "Wind that another boat's sail has slowed and bent. It lies behind and to leeward of every boat, and it makes you slower and lower. Clear air is wind that no sail has touched.\nAt the club: aire sucio."}, "G19": {"t": "Transit", "b": "Two things lined up, such as the pin end and a building on shore behind it. When they line up, you know you are on the start line.\nAt the club: enfilación."}, "G20": {"t": "Gap", "b": "Space on the start line, usually just below you, that lets you bear away and speed up before the gun.\nAt the club: hueco."}, "G21": {"t": "Pin end", "b": "The end of the start line marked by a buoy, opposite the committee boat.\nAt the club: boya de salida."}, "G22": {"t": "Telltales", "b": "The small strips of wool or tape on your sail. They show how the air flows over it. When they stream back on both sides, the sail is working."}, "G23": {"t": "Bear away", "b": "To turn the bow away from the wind.\nAt the club: arribar."}, "G24": {"t": "Head up", "b": "To turn the bow towards the wind. Also called luffing.\nAt the club: orzar."}, "G25": {"t": "Heading", "b": "The direction your bow points, as a number of degrees on the compass, from 0 to 360. Turn the bow to the right and the number gets bigger.\nAt the club: rumbo."}, "G26": {"t": "Lull", "b": "A patch of lighter wind, the opposite of a gust. It usually shows as smoother, paler water."}, "G28": {"t": "Normal", "b": "The direction your bow points, close-hauled on one tack, when the wind is in the middle of its swings. Higher than normal you are lifted. Lower than normal you are headed. Each tack has its own normal, and both move when the middle of the wind moves."}, "G29": {"t": "Pressure", "b": "The strength of the wind. More pressure means more wind. Sailors say “more pressure on the left” when there is more wind on the left of the course."}, "G30": {"t": "Gust lane", "b": "A lane of stronger wind that stays in about the same place, usually where the wind pours through a gap in the land or round a point. Gusts keep coming down it for as long as the wind keeps its direction."}, "G31": {"t": "Wind shadow", "b": "The area of light, patchy wind behind something that blocks the wind: high land, buildings, trees or a big boat. A sail makes one too, and that one is called dirty air."}, "G32": {"t": "Heel", "b": "To lean over sideways. A gust makes a boat heel more, until the sailor hikes harder or eases the sheet.\nAt the club: escorar."}, "G33": {"t": "Long tack", "b": "When the mark is not straight upwind, one tack points closer to it than the other, and you have to sail more of that tack to get there. That is the long tack. The other one is the short tack."}, "G34": {"t": "Corner", "b": "The far left or far right of the course, out at a layline or beyond it. From a corner you have one way to the mark and no choices left."}, "G35": {"t": "Current", "b": "Water that is moving as a whole, like a slow river, carrying everything that floats with it. Rivers and tides make it, and so does a wind that has blown across a bay for hours.\nAt the club: corriente."}, "G36": {"t": "Chop", "b": "Short, steep waves close together, made by the wind blowing now. Longer, smoother waves that have travelled from far away are called swell."}, "G37": {"t": "Favoured end", "b": "The end of the start line that is further upwind. A boat starting there is higher up the ladder, closer to the mark along the wind, than a boat at the other end. How far ahead it is changes with every shift, and a big shift can move it to the other end."}, "G38": {"t": "Committee boat", "b": "The anchored boat at one end of the start line, usually the right-hand end when you look upwind, from which the race officer runs the start. The other end is the pin.\nAt the club: barco del comité."}, "G39": {"t": "Front row", "b": "The boats that are right on the line at the gun with nobody ahead of them. The second row starts a length or two behind, in their dirty air."}, "G40": {"t": "Head to wind", "b": "Pointing the bow straight into the wind, so that the sail flaps down the middle of the boat and the boat stops.\nAt the club: proa al viento."}, "G41": {"t": "Lane", "b": "The strip of clear wind you sail in after the start, with room below you to bear away and room above you to point. The boats on either side of you make it or close it."}, "G42": {"t": "Sag", "b": "When the boats in the middle of a long start line hang back behind the real line, because nobody there can see the ends. The front row in the middle can be two or three boat lengths behind the line without knowing it."}, "G43": {"t": "General recall", "b": "When many boats are over the line at the gun, the race committee can call the whole fleet back and start the race again. Chapter 12 explains the signal and what it means for a boat that was over."}, "G44": {"t": "Warning signal", "b": "The flag and sound that begin the starting sequence, normally five minutes before the gun. Your watch starts here, and the flags, not your guess, give the time."}, "G45": {"t": "Over early", "b": "Being over the start line when the gun goes: part of your hull on the course side of the line. Chapter 12 explains the signals and what you have to do about it."}, "G46": {"t": "Runway", "b": "The gap below you, a length or two, seen as the space you need: open water to bear away into and reach full speed before the gun, with about three boat lengths of sailing to the line."}, "G47": {"t": "The triangle", "b": "The area of water with the pin, the committee boat and the first mark at its corners. The Z, U and black flags keep you out of it in the last minute before the gun."}, "G48": {"t": "Individual recall", "b": "The signal that at least one boat was over the line at the gun: flag X on the committee boat with one sound. It does not say which boat."}, "G49": {"t": "Extension", "b": "The start line continued in a straight line beyond the pin or beyond the committee boat. Under the I flag, being over an extension in the last minute counts like being over the line."}, "G50": {"t": "Pinch", "b": "To point higher than the boat wants, so that the front of the sail goes soft and the boat slows down. A little pinching for a few seconds can hold you up; a long pinch is slow."}, "G51": {"t": "Foot", "b": "To sail a little lower than your best angle, with the sail full and the boat fast. Footing gives up a little height for speed, and for the room to get out from under a boat close to windward. Bearing away brings you closer to any boat to leeward, so check the water below you first."}, "G52": {"t": "Duck", "b": "To bear away and pass behind the stern of a boat that has right of way, instead of crossing in front of her. A duck costs about a boat length."}, "G53": {"t": "Lee bow", "b": "A boat close ahead of you and to leeward. The wind leaving her sail reaches you bent, like a header, so you point lower and go slower. Being lee-bowed is the usual way a lane dies from below."}, "G54": {"t": "Zone", "b": "The water within three boat lengths of a mark, where the mark rules begin to apply. Chapter 17 explains what changes when you enter it."}, "G55": {"t": "Cover", "b": "Sailing so that a rival behind you cannot get past without passing through your position. A loose cover keeps you between the rival and the mark with your own clear air. A tight cover sits on one boat's wind and slows you both."}, "G56": {"t": "Outside boat", "b": "The boat furthest out to one side of the fleet. She has the most leverage on everyone, so the next shift gains or loses her the most, and from a corner she has only one way to the mark."}, "G57": {"t": "Wave through", "b": "On starboard, letting a port-tack boat cross ahead of you instead of making her tack or duck: you bear away a little and call to her, early, before she has started to duck. You do it when her tacking under your bow would be worse for you than her crossing."}, "G58": {"t": "Lee-bow tack", "b": "A tack that ends with you just ahead of and to leeward of a boat you were meeting on the opposite tack, so that now, on the same tack, she is in your bent wind and has to tack or fall back. It needs room to finish the tack and give her room to keep clear."}, "G59": {"t": "Fetch", "b": "To be able to pass to windward of the mark, leaving it on the side the course requires (in this book to port), without another tack. A boat on the layline fetches the mark; a boat below it does not, and has to tack twice more."}, "G60": {"t": "Mark-room", "b": "The room a boat is owed at a mark: room to sail to it and to round it without being squeezed out. Inside the zone, a boat that was overlapped on the inside when the first of them reached the zone, or clear ahead at that moment, is given mark-room by the other boat on the same tack. On a beat, boats on opposite tacks owe each other none."}, "G61": {"t": "Overlap", "b": "Two boats are overlapped when neither is wholly behind the other: part of one boat is level with part of the other. A boat completely behind is clear astern, and the one in front is clear ahead. It is measured from a line across the very back of the boat ahead, rudder and boom included; two boats that are not level still count as overlapped if a boat between them is overlapped with both; and on a beat, boats on opposite tacks are never overlapped."}, "G62": {"t": "Keep clear", "b": "What the boat without right of way has to do: stay out of the way so well that the other boat can sail her course without having to do anything, and, when the two are overlapped, can turn either way without touching you. Keeping clear is not avoiding a collision at the last second; it is leaving room."}, "G63": {"t": "Right of way", "b": "The boat the other one has to keep clear of. Starboard over port, leeward over windward, clear ahead over clear astern. It comes with duties: avoid contact if you reasonably can, and give room when you change course."}, "G64": {"t": "Penalty turns", "b": "The way to pay for a rule about another boat that you may have broken, at once and on the water: two full turns, each with a tack and a gybe, taken well clear of other boats; one turn for touching a mark. Taken promptly, they keep you in the race."}, "G65": {"t": "Sprit", "b": "The pole that runs from the mast up to the top back corner of the sail (the peak) and holds it up. More sprit tension pulls the peak out and changes the shape of the top of the sail; how much to use depends on your sail, so follow its maker's guide."}, "G66": {"t": "Vang", "b": "The control that holds the boom down. It keeps the back edge of the sail from opening when you ease the sheet, so it matters most in strong wind and in gusts. Some sailors call it the kicker."}, "G67": {"t": "Outhaul", "b": "The control at the back end of the boom that pulls the bottom back corner of the sail (the clew) out. Tighter makes the bottom of the sail flatter; looser makes it fuller."}, "G68": {"t": "Leech", "b": "The back edge of the sail, from the top back corner down to the end of the boom. The telltale on the top batten shows whether air is flowing off it or stalling behind it."}, "G69": {"t": "Modes", "b": "Your gears upwind. Speed mode: a little lower, both luff telltales streaming, for acceleration. VMG mode: the normal fast angle; VMG is short for velocity made good, the part of your speed that takes you straight upwind, which is usually, not always, towards the windward mark; VMG mode is the angle where that part is biggest. Point mode: a little higher, for a lane. Pinch: higher still, only for a few seconds. You change gear on purpose, like a cyclist on a hill."}, "G70": {"t": "Feather", "b": "A tiny turn towards the wind in a gust, once the boat is fast and flat, so that the sail carries a little less power and you climb a little. Tiny is the word: a big turn or a lot of rudder slows you down."}, "G71": {"t": "In irons", "b": "Stopped head to wind with the sail flapping and the boat not answering the tiller. It happens when you point too high for too long, or stall in a tack in strong wind. To get out, look behind you first: while you are moving backwards you keep clear of every other boat, even on starboard. Then push the boom out to one side with your hand, so that the wind fills the sail from the wrong side and the boat moves backwards. Hold the tiller over to the same side as the boom. The stern swings away from that side and the bow swings towards it. When the bow has swung away from the wind and the wind comes over the side away from the boom, centre the tiller and sheet in. It costs lengths."}, "G72": {"t": "Race card", "b": "One small card for one race. Before the start you fill in what you see, your one first move and the start plan from chapter 9; after the race you fill in how the start went, one honest reason and one thing for next time; then you draw the first beat. Chapter 21 shows it; chapter 23 keeps the score."}, "G73": {"t": "Corridor", "b": "A strip of the course running from the line to the mark: the left corridor, the middle, the right. In practice you sail each one on purpose, so that you learn what each side does before you have to choose one in a race."}, "G74": {"t": "Scorecard", "b": "A sheet with eight parts of a first beat, each scored 0, 1 or 2 after a race: the look before the start, the start plan, the start itself, lanes, shifts and pressure, risk, the layline and the mark, and the rules. It scores how you raced, not where you finished. Chapter 23 shows it."}, "G75": {"t": "Strategy", "b": "Your plan for the beat, made from the wind, the water and the course: which side, which tack first, how far from the middle. It would be the same plan if you were the only boat on the course. Chapter 6 separates it from tactics, and chapters 7 and 8 show how to make it."}, "G76": {"t": "Tactics", "b": "What you do about the boats near you while you carry out the plan: cross, tack or duck, lee-bow, hold a lane or leave it, cover. Tactics serve the strategy; a tactical move that gives away the plan is a loss even when it wins the meeting. Chapters 13, 15 and 16 are the tactics chapters."}, "G77": {"t": "Leading group", "b": "The bunch of boats that reaches the first mark first. Arriving in it is the real objective of the start and the first beat; winning the line is only a way of getting there, and not the only one."}, "G78": {"t": "Skew", "b": "A course is skewed when the first mark is not straight upwind of the start line but off to one side. Then one tack points closer to the mark than the other and you sail more of it: that is the long tack. Section 7.4 shows how to see the skew before the start."}, "G79": {"t": "Split test", "b": "A test before the start with a partner of about your speed: from one point you sail off on opposite tacks for the same few minutes, both tack back, and whoever crosses ahead sailed through the better wind or water. Section 7.3 shows how to do it and what it can and cannot tell you."}, "G80": {"t": "Plan B", "b": "The plan you fall back on when Plan A is cancelled: a second place to start and a second side, chosen before the start, usually the middle. Chapter 8 shows how to choose it and the trigger that switches to it."}, "G81": {"t": "Trigger", "b": "The one thing that, if it happens, cancels Plan A and starts Plan B: no gap at one minute, or the sign that the day has changed. It is chosen and said out loud before the start, so that the change is a decision and not a panic."}, "G27": {"t": "Windward and leeward", "b": "Windward is the side the wind comes from. Leeward is the side it blows towards. A boat to windward of you is between you and the wind.\nAt the club: barlovento, sotavento."}}, "LOCAL": {"B1": {"t": "Why boats on the same rung are level", "b": "Sailing upwind, every Optimist zigzags at about the same angle to the wind on either tack. So each metre you sail lifts you up the ladder by the same amount, whether you are on port or on starboard.\nThat means being out on the left or the right changes nothing on its own while the wind stays steady. Only the rung you are on counts. What changes the order is the wind changing direction, which is the next section."}, "B2": {"t": "Why the side the wind shifted to gains", "b": "Think of the rung you were both on as a ruler, turning about its middle. When the wind shifts right the ruler turns with it: its right end drops back behind the right-hand boat, and its left end swings up in front of the left-hand boat.\nThe right-hand boat is now above the ruler, further up the ladder. The left-hand boat is below it. Neither boat moved. The ladder did."}, "B3": {"t": "Why the lifted tack wins on a swinging day", "b": "On a swinging day the wind keeps coming back to its middle direction, so the ladder that counts is the one set by that middle wind.\nWhen you are lifted, your bow points closer to straight up that middle ladder, so you climb it faster than on the headed tack. Tack every time you are headed and you are always on the tack that climbs fastest. You are also always sailing towards the side the next swing will come from."}, "B4": {"t": "Why the gain grows with the distance apart", "b": "Go back to the ruler turning about its middle. A point near the middle hardly moves when the ruler turns. A point near the end moves a lot.\nThe rung is the ruler. So the further a boat is from the middle, the further its rung moves when the wind turns, and the bigger the gain or the loss."}, "B5": {"t": "Why no shift helps you on the layline", "b": "Before the layline, on a swinging day, a shift always gives you something to do: a header means tack onto the better course, and a lift means keep going on a better course.\nOn the layline you have used up your choices. A lift turns your bow above the mark: you could have tacked earlier, so some of the metres you have already sailed were wasted. A header turns your bow below the mark, so you need two more tacks to get there. A lift is wasted and a header hurts."}, "B6": {"t": "Why one shift is a lift on one tack and a header on the other", "b": "As you look up the course, a boat on starboard tack points to the left of the wind and a boat on port tack points to the right of it, each by about 45 degrees.\nWhen the wind turns to the right, both boats turn to the right with it. The starboard boat was pointing left of straight upwind, so turning right brings its bow closer to straight upwind: a lift. The port boat was pointing right of straight upwind, so turning right takes its bow further away: a header. A shift to the left does the opposite."}, "B7": {"t": "Why your bow shows the shift", "b": "You steer by the sail, and the sail has to sit at the same angle to the wind to work. So when the wind turns 10 degrees, you turn 10 degrees to keep the sail working.\nThe mark has not moved. So the gap between your bow and the mark has changed by the size of the shift, and that is what you see."}, "B8": {"t": "Why the boats ahead get the new wind first", "b": "The wind blows down the course from the mark towards you, and it carries most of its changes with it. A new direction or a gust reaches the top of the course first and then moves down, at about the speed of the wind.\nIn a moderate breeze, a boat 50 metres further up the course gets the change several seconds before you do. A bend in the wind near the shore is different: it stays where it is."}, "B9": {"t": "Why a drop in the wind feels like a header", "b": "The wind you feel on the boat is the real wind mixed with the wind your own speed makes, and that second part always comes from straight ahead.\nWhen the real wind drops, the boat keeps its speed for a moment. For that moment, more of the wind you feel comes from ahead, exactly as it does in a header. As the boat slows down, the feeling goes away again."}, "B10": {"t": "Why a short swing is not worth a tack", "b": "A tack costs a boat length or two, because the boat slows down while it turns.\nOn the better tack you win that back slowly: roughly one boat length every ten seconds on a small shift, and faster on a big one. So a shift has to last a good while after your tack before the tack has paid for itself. A swing that is gone in five seconds never does."}, "B11": {"t": "Why right means lifted on starboard and headed on port", "b": "On starboard tack the wind is on your right, so turning right is heading up. A lift lets you do that, so when you are lifted your bow is to the right of your point.\nOn port tack the wind is on your left, so turning right is bearing away. That is what a header makes you do, so there a bow to the right of your point means headed.\nA compass says the same in numbers. They get bigger as the bow turns right, so a number bigger than normal means lifted on starboard and headed on port."}, "B12": {"t": "Why your point must be far away", "b": "Your point only slides while you sail on the other tack, because then you are moving sideways to it. After 300 metres on the other tack, a point 2 kilometres away sits about 8 degrees from where it was: as much as a whole shift. A point 10 kilometres away has moved by less than 2 degrees.\nWith a point ten times as far away as the mark, the slide is about 4 degrees at most, at the top of the beat. It always slides the same way. So as the beat goes on, your point shows a little more lift than there really is, on both tacks."}, "B13": {"t": "Why more wind looks darker", "b": "Calm water is like a mirror. It reflects the bright sky, so it looks pale and shiny.\nWind roughens the water with ripples. Each ripple is a tiny slope, and the slopes facing you reflect less of the bright sky and let you see more of the dark water underneath. The more wind, the more ripples, and the darker the patch looks."}, "B14": {"t": "Why the wind comes in patches", "b": "Higher up, the wind is far from the water that slows it, so it blows faster there. Close to the water it is slowed by rubbing on the surface.\nBig, slow swirls in the air keep mixing the two. Where a swirl brings fast air down to the water, you get a gust. Where slow air drifts up, you get a lull. The swirls are carried along by the wind, so the patches move down the course with it."}, "B15": {"t": "Why every 20 seconds", "b": "In a breeze of about 10 knots a patch of wind moves down the course at roughly 5 metres every second: about 100 metres in 20 seconds. You sail upwind towards it at the same time, so it comes about 120 metres closer.\nSo a look that reaches 150 metres or more up the course every 20 seconds sees almost every gust a few seconds before it arrives. In more wind the patches come faster, and the look has to reach further. Look less often and some arrive without warning. Look all the time and you stop sailing the boat well."}, "B16": {"t": "Why more wind wins", "b": "More wind pushes the sail harder, so the boat goes faster. A faster boat also goes higher: the daggerboard grips the water better, so the boat slips sideways less. And when a gust first hits, the wind you feel swings a little further back, which lets you head up. That part is not a wind shift: when the gust has passed, the wind you feel swings forward again.\nAn example: you sail at 3 knots and a boat in a gust sails at 3.5. After two minutes it is about 30 metres further on: 13 boat lengths."}, "B17": {"t": "Why ripples tell the truth", "b": "Ripples are made by the wind blowing on the water. More wind makes more ripples, and they appear the moment a gust arrives.\nColour can come from many things: a cloud's shadow, deep water, weed, or a dark hill reflected in smooth water. Those change the colour of the water, not its ripples. So a patch that is only darker may have no extra wind at all."}, "B18": {"t": "Why the wind pours through the valleys", "b": "Air flowing over the land is like a stream flowing past rocks. Hills get in its way, so it squeezes through the gaps between them and speeds up there.\nWhen it reaches the water it spreads out in a fan. The land does not move, so the lanes stay in about the same place for as long as the wind blows from the same direction."}, "B19": {"t": "Why the wind is light under high land", "b": "Land, buildings and trees block the wind like a wall. Behind them the air tumbles and slows down, and it needs a long way to get its speed back.\nAs a rough guide, the wind stays weak for up to about ten times the height of whatever is in the way, counted from it. A steep hill 50 metres high right on the shore can spoil the wind 500 metres out over the water."}, "B20": {"t": "Why the wind swings back and forth", "b": "Wind does not flow like a smooth river. It comes in big swirls, a few hundred metres to a kilometre or more across, carried along with the wind. As a swirl passes you, the wind at your sail turns a little one way, then back, then a little the other way.\nOn an ordinary day each swing is a few degrees to 15 degrees and lasts a few minutes. The middle of all the swings is the wind’s real direction for the day, and where your bow points in that wind is your normal."}, "B21": {"t": "Why you take the header first on a one-way day", "b": "The ladder always lies across the wind. On a one-way day the wind keeps turning, so the ladder keeps turning the same way, and the boats on the side it turns towards keep gaining.\nA boat that goes to that side early is on the lifted tack for the long last leg to the mark, when the shift has grown biggest. A boat that tacks on the first header sails away from that side, and meets the biggest part of the shift on the wrong tack."}, "B22": {"t": "Why the embat turns to the right", "b": "The Earth turns, and in the northern half of the world that pushes moving air a little to the right. A wind that starts afresh every day, like the embat, is pushed further and further round the longer it blows.\nThe embat starts by blowing straight from the sea onto the land. As the afternoon goes on, it is bent further and further to the right, until it blows at a slant to the shore. Hills and headlands can hold it in one direction, so each bay has its own version of this."}, "B23": {"t": "Why the embat is steady", "b": "The embat is driven by one thing: the land is warmer than the sea. That difference changes slowly through the day, so the wind changes slowly too.\nThe air also comes in over the smooth sea, where nothing stirs it into big swirls, so it carries fewer gusts and lulls than wind that has crossed the land."}, "B24": {"t": "Why more wind beats a better angle in an Optimist", "b": "When the wind is shifted 5 degrees, the lifted tack points 10 degrees closer to the mark than the headed tack, and climbs the ladder about one fifth faster.\nMore wind can do more. An Optimist is small and light, so its speed depends a lot on the strength of the wind. In light wind, two more knots of wind can make you about a quarter faster, and you point a little higher too (section 1.3). The gain lasts for as long as you stay in the wind. In a good breeze every boat is already going fast, and the same two knots add much less. That is when the angle counts for more.\nAnd a big shift, 10 degrees or more, makes the lifted tack nearly half as fast again as the headed one. Then only a lot more wind beats it."}, "B25": {"t": "Why the middle keeps your choices", "b": "From the middle of the course you can go either way, so whichever way the next swing comes, you can use it.\nAt a layline you have run out of choices: a lift is wasted, because you were already going to reach the mark, and a header means two more tacks. And the further you are from the other boats, the more one shift moves you up or down the ladder against them, for better or for worse."}, "B26": {"t": "Why a gust can bring its own direction", "b": "A gust is a parcel of faster air that comes down from higher up, where the wind blows from a slightly different direction. So a gust often arrives with a direction of its own, and when it passes, the old direction comes back.\nIn the northern half of the world the air from higher up usually blows from a little further to the right. So a gust often gives a small lift on starboard and a small header on port, and both go away with the gust. The extra height a gust gives you through your own speed (section 1.3) is a different thing, and comes on both tacks."}, "B27": {"t": "Why the long tack keeps you near the middle", "b": "When the mark is off to one side of straight upwind, you need more of one tack than the other to get there. That tack points closer to the line towards the mark.\nSail the short tack first and you move away from that line, towards one side of the course. Sail the long tack first and you move along it, nearer the mark and nearer the middle, with the short tack kept for later."}, "B28": {"t": "Why the current carries every boat the same", "b": "A current moves the whole body of water, and everything floating in it moves with it, at the same speed and in the same direction. Your boat sails through the water the way it always does. It is the water itself that is moving underneath.\nSo between two boats on the same water nothing changes: whoever was ahead stays ahead. What changes is where the water takes you both compared with the marks, which are anchored to the bottom."}, "B29": {"t": "Why a difference in current pays", "b": "Water rubs on the bottom, so close to the shore and over shallow banks it moves slowly, while in deep water and in channels it runs fastest. It also speeds up where it is squeezed round a point or through a gap.\nTwo boats on different water are on different rugs. A boat with half a knot less current against it gains about 15 metres on the other every minute."}, "B30": {"t": "Why an anchored mark shows the current", "b": "An anchored mark is held to the bottom, so the water has to flow past it. On the side the water comes from it piles up a little, and on the other side it leaves a small wake, like a rock in a stream.\nStill water leaves nothing. The faster the flow, the clearer the wake."}, "B31": {"t": "Why current pushes you over the line", "b": "A boat that is not moving through the water still moves over the ground, because the water carries it. In a current of one knot that is about half a metre every second: 13 boat lengths in a minute, all of it towards the course.\nThe line is anchored, so it does not move with you. Waiting in your usual place, you cross it before the gun without sailing a metre."}, "B32": {"t": "Why waves slow an Optimist", "b": "Going up the face of a wave is going uphill, and the bow has to push water aside as it climbs. The boat slows, and as it slows it slips sideways.\nThe boat is light and short, so it pitches with every wave, and each pitch rocks the sail through the air, so the sail is seldom at its best angle."}, "B33": {"t": "Why wind against current makes steeper waves", "b": "Waves are made by the wind and travel with it. When the water underneath flows the other way, the waves are slowed down, so they bunch up: closer together and steeper.\nWith wind and current running the same way, the waves are stretched out and flatter."}, "B34": {"t": "Why one tack meets the waves better", "b": "Waves follow the wind that made them, but slowly. When the wind shifts, the waves keep coming from the old direction for a while, and swell from far away may come from somewhere else again.\nClose-hauled, you sail about 45 degrees from the wind on either tack. If the waves come from the left of the wind, starboard tack meets them nearly head-on and port tack crosses them at a slant. The slanting tack climbs each wave more gently, so it keeps its speed."}, "B35": {"t": "Why clear air beats a small bias", "b": "A boat in dirty air sails slower and points lower. Ten per cent slower for the first minute, at three knots, is about nine metres: four boat lengths, and the lost height comes on top.\nAn end that is one or two boat lengths further upwind cannot pay for that. Only an end worth many boat lengths can, and only for the boat that gets the front row there."}, "B36": {"t": "Why one end of the line is ahead", "b": "The ladder rungs run straight across the wind (chapter 3). If the line is tilted against the rungs, one end sits on a higher rung than the other, and a boat starting there begins the beat that much further up.\nHow much depends on the length of the line and the tilt. On a line 200 metres long, a tilt of 10 degrees puts the upwind end about 35 metres ahead: 15 boat lengths. A tilt of 5 degrees is still about 17 metres, 7 or 8 boat lengths.\nOnly the boat right at the end gets all of it: a boat a quarter of the way along from that end gets three quarters, a boat in the middle half."}, "B37": {"t": "Why the favoured end is slow for most boats", "b": "Every sailor can do the head-to-wind test, so the favoured end draws a crowd. In a crowd each sail puts dirty air on the boats behind and to leeward, nobody has a gap to bear away into, and the end mark takes away the room to leeward.\nOne boat at the very end gets clear air and the full bias. The boats packed next to it get the bias and lose it again in the first minute, slow and low."}, "B38": {"t": "Why the end nearest your side gets you there", "b": "Every boat starts on starboard tack, heading left. From the pin end you are already the furthest boat to the left, and the boats to windward of you must keep clear, so you can hold starboard towards that side for as long as you want.\nFrom the committee boat you are the furthest to the right. Tack onto port a few lengths after the gun and there is nobody to cross, as long as no boat is close beside you to windward or right behind you. From the wrong end, the whole fleet lies between you and your side."}, "B39": {"t": "Why full speed at the gun is worth more than a boat length", "b": "An Optimist needs about ten seconds to get from a standstill to full speed. A boat still slow at the gun drops a length or two behind the boats beside it in those ten seconds.\nBy then it sits behind and to leeward of them, in their dirty air, and dirty air keeps it slow. The gap is what lets you bear away and be at full speed before the gun, not after it."}, "B40": {"t": "Why the middle of the line is harder to judge", "b": "From the middle you cannot see whether you are on the line: the pin and the committee boat are far away on either side, and the boats around you are all judging by each other. So the middle of the fleet drifts back from the line without noticing, and the boats that go up to the real line gain free lengths.\nA transit, two things lined up through the pin, is the only honest answer. Chapter 10 shows how."}, "B41": {"t": "Why two lengths at the gun are worth so much", "b": "A boat that starts two lengths ahead of the row beside it has no sail in front of her: clear air from the first second. The row behind her is in her dirty air, and dirty air costs about four lengths in the first minute (chapter 9).\nSo the two lengths at the gun are six after a minute. That is what the boat on the real line takes from forty boats that sagged, without being faster than any of them."}, "B42": {"t": "Why a transit tells you where the line is", "b": "Three points that line up lie on one straight line. The two ends of the start line and the shore mark behind one of them line up only for a boat that is on the line itself. From behind the line, the far end shows on the upwind side of the shore mark. From over the line, it shows on the downwind side.\nThe nearer you are to that end, the bigger the shift and the easier the test. Two lengths behind the line, halfway along a 200 metre line, the end sits about two fingers' width off the shore mark at arm's length; one length behind, about one finger."}, "B43": {"t": "Why there is never room for everyone", "b": "An Optimist is 2.3 metres long. Seventy boats side by side, each with a one-length gap to bear away into, need more than 300 metres of line, half as much again as the 200 metre line of chapter 9. So the fleet cannot all be in the front row.\nAnd it never spreads out evenly. It piles where the favoured end is, where the committee boat is, and where the best sailors go. A pile three deep at one end leaves the middle thin, and the thin place is where a front-row start costs nothing."}, "B44": {"t": "Why a pile is a bet", "b": "In a pile of ten boats at the favoured end, one boat wins the end: clear air and the full gain. The nine around it have no gap to bear away into, so they cross the line slowly, in each other's dirty air, and some of them are over. Ten boats, one good start.\nFive lengths along the line from that end costs less than one length of the end's gain, even on a line tilted ten degrees (chapter 9). A minute of dirty air costs about four. So the front row five lengths away beats the pile nine times in ten."}, "B45": {"t": "Why your neighbours make the lane", "b": "Dirty air lies behind and to leeward of every sail, so a boat ahead and to windward of you sails you into hers. A boat close to leeward and a little ahead does something else: the wind leaving her sail reaches you bent, as a header, so you point lower and slow down. Sailors call it the lee bow.\nClear air needs space on both sides: a windward boat that is level with you or behind, and a leeward boat that is level or behind, or two lengths away. That strip is your lane. The boats make it, not the water."}, "B46": {"t": "Why the first move is decided before the start", "b": "In the ten seconds before the gun your eyes are needed for the line, the gap below you and the boat above you; after it, for the sail, the water ahead and the boats close to you. There is no time to think about the beat, so a move decided then is late.\nA tack from the middle also has a price that is easy to forget. Every boat to your right is on starboard, so on port you duck each one you cannot cross, and each duck costs about a length. Twenty boats to cross can mean ten or more ducks. From the committee boat end there is nobody to cross."}, "B47": {"t": "Why a routine helps in the last minute", "b": "In the last minute your attention goes to three things and nothing else: the line, the gap and the boat above you. Anything you still have to decide then competes with them, and loses.\nA routine moves every decision to the minute that has time for it: the card between five and four, the run-up between four and three, the neighbours between three and two. The same words every time also tell you at once what you skipped: reach two minutes without having said “neighbours” and you know."}, "B48": {"t": "Why the favoured end moves with the wind", "b": "The favoured end is the end on the higher ladder rung, and the rungs run straight across the wind (chapter 3). When the wind shifts, the rungs turn with it and the line stays where it is.\nOn a 200 metre line a shift of five degrees moves one end about 17 metres up or down the ladder, seven or eight boat lengths (chapter 9). A ten degree shift on a line that was square makes one end 15 lengths favoured. The same shift the other way on a line that favoured the pin can make it square, or favour the boat."}, "B49": {"t": "Why gaps open late", "b": "A boat waiting on the line is nearly stopped, and a stopped boat cannot steer: the rudder only works with water flowing past it. So the waiting boats drift, downwind and sideways, and the spaces between them change every few seconds.\nAdd the boats sagging back because they cannot see the line, and the boats leaving a pile they counted too big, and the row at one minute is not the row at two. The ends of the piles open most, because that is where the leavers come from."}, "B50": {"t": "Why the starboard approach is the safe one", "b": "On starboard tack every port-tack boat keeps clear of you {S1}, and once you are in the hole the boat above you is the windward boat and keeps clear of you {S2}. The boat below you is the leeward boat: you keep clear of her. Coming in on starboard you keep clear of any boat you are still clear astern of {S7}. The moment you are alongside a boat, below her, she keeps clear of you {S2}, but you must first give her room to do it {S5}.\nComing in on port you have none: you keep clear of the whole row, and the tack into the hole takes time and room that the hole may not have."}, "B51": {"t": "Why a boat below you can take your gap", "b": "Rights on the line go to the leeward boat: a boat to windward keeps clear {S2}. The moment her bow passes your stern on your leeward side you are overlapped: she is the leeward boat and you are the one keeping clear. She has your runway, and she can hold you up until the gun.\nThat is why the door is closed early, with a small bear-away while her bow is still behind your stern, and not with a fight once she is in."}, "B52": {"t": "Why the runway is about three lengths", "b": "From nearly stopped an Optimist takes about ten seconds to reach full speed (chapter 9). Full speed close-hauled is about three knots, one and a half metres a second, but you spend those ten seconds getting there, so you cover about half of fifteen metres: seven or eight metres, about three boat lengths.\nBearing away helps, because the boat speeds up faster with the wind further round, and that is why the gap below you is the runway and not the water ahead. In light wind full speed is lower and takes longer to reach: the lengths are fewer, the seconds more, so the count starts earlier."}, "B53": {"t": "Why the escape is chosen at thirty seconds", "b": "Every escape needs room and speed. Out the back needs a hole behind the boat below you. The tack out needs room above you and no starboard boat close behind. Both need a boat that is still moving, because a stopped boat cannot turn.\nAt thirty seconds the row still has holes and you still have steerage. At five seconds the row is solid, every boat is bearing away at once, and there is nowhere to go and no way to get there."}, "B54": {"t": "Why flag X does not tell you who was over", "b": "The race committee sights along the line from the committee boat, and at the gun it sees which bows are over. It then shows one flag for everyone and sounds once. Nothing on that flag says which boats it saw.\nSo every boat near the line has to decide for itself. The committee writes the sail numbers down, and a boat that was over and does not go back is scored as if it had never started. That is why the honest answer is the cheap one."}, "B55": {"t": "Why the fleet sags more under the I flag", "b": "Under P a boat that finds itself over at thirty seconds drops back a length and is fine. Under I the same boat has to sail to an end, round it, and come back to its place: from the middle of a long line that is two or three minutes, so the start is lost.\nEvery sailor knows it, so every sailor keeps a little more margin, and the middle, which cannot see the line anyway (chapter 10), keeps the most. The row sags, and the boat with a transit gains."}, "B56": {"t": "Why twenty per cent costs about fourteen places", "b": "The Z flag penalty is twenty per cent of the score for not finishing, added to your score; the notice of race or the sailing instructions can set a different number. In a regatta of seventy boats not finishing scores seventy-one points, so the penalty is a little over fourteen points: fifth place scores like nineteenth, tenth place like twenty-fourth. It can never make your score worse than the score for not finishing. In a bigger regatta it is usually more; the sailing instructions say how the boats are counted.\nIt is added after the race, so it cannot be sailed off. A boat that wins the pin under Z and is identified in the triangle has won the pin and lost about fourteen places."}, "B57": {"t": "Why there is no room at a starting mark", "b": "At a mark of the course the rule book gives the inside boat room to round it (chapter 18), so that a boat cannot be pushed into the mark. If that applied at the start, every boat coming in to windward at the committee boat could claim room from the boat below, and the leeward boat would have to make space for her in the last seconds.\nThe rules take the mark rules away at a starting mark surrounded by water, from the approach until the mark is astern. So the ordinary rule holds: the windward boat keeps clear, and the boat that arrived late above you has nothing to ask for."}, "B58": {"t": "Why speed comes before everything in the first minute", "b": "A slow boat cannot point: the daggerboard and the rudder only work with water flowing past them, so a boat at half speed slides sideways when you try to point her, and loses more. Speed first, then height.\nAnd every boat around you is fast. A boat ten per cent slower for a minute loses about four lengths (chapter 9), and at the start four lengths is the difference between the front row and the second row. The first minute is where the start is won or given back."}, "B59": {"t": "Why a lane dies", "b": "A lane dies from below or from above. From below: a boat close ahead and to leeward bends the wind off her sail into yours, so you point lower and slow down, and as she gains she bends it more. From above: a boat ahead and to windward drops down towards your track and her dirty air falls on you.\nBoth get worse by themselves. The slower you go, the more sideways you slide, and the closer you come to the boat below. That is why a dying lane does not recover by waiting."}, "B60": {"t": "Why leaving early costs less", "b": "Dirty air costs about four lengths a minute, about a length every fifteen seconds (chapter 9). A tack costs a length or two (chapter 3). So after fifteen to thirty seconds in a lane that is clearly dying you have already lost what the tack would have cost, and every second after that is a loss you chose.\nSailors wait because the next ten seconds might get better. They rarely do: a lane dies because of where the boats are, and the boats are not going away."}, "B61": {"t": "Why the first escape to the right needs a hole behind you", "b": "Every boat to your right after the start is on starboard, heading left towards you, and on port you keep clear of each one {S1}. A tack with a wall of them close behind means a duck for every boat, about a length each.\nA hole behind you, two or three lengths with no bow in it, is a tack you can make without ducking anyone, and the first hole is usually the one behind the boat to windward of you once she has sailed on a little."}, "B62": {"t": "Why holding starboard too long is a dead end", "b": "Holding starboard takes you left. Hold it long enough and you reach the left layline (chapter 17), where you must tack onto port for the mark. The boats that tacked earlier went right and come back towards the mark on starboard, and now you are the one keeping clear, of every one of them {S1}.\nAnd a boat close above you leaves you no room to tack at all. So the starboard lane is kept with an eye on two things: the layline ahead and the room to windward."}, "B63": {"t": "Why the panic tack loses", "b": "A tack straight after a poor start puts you on port in the first ten seconds, when every boat behind you is on starboard, fast and close. You keep clear of all of them {S1}: a duck each, a length each, and when you come back on starboard you are still in the dirty air of the boats ahead and above you.\nIt also gives up the side you chose before the start for no reason connected with the wind. A poor start is a few lengths. The panic tack makes it many."}, "B64": {"t": "Why going out to a side is only safe when you are sure", "b": "A shift gains or loses between two boats in proportion to the sideways distance between them, their leverage (chapter 3). A boat 100 metres to one side of you gains or loses about 17 metres on a ten degree shift, seven or eight boat lengths; two boats a length apart gain or lose almost nothing.\nSo going out to a side is a bet whose size you choose. When the advantage there is strong and certain, more wind you can see or a one-way day, the bet is worth making big. When it is a guess, keep the leverage small and stay near the middle, where any shift costs little."}, "B65": {"t": "Why the boats on your rung tell the truth", "b": "The leader far ahead is in different wind: the gust that reached her has not reached you, the shift she is on may be over by the time it arrives. Comparing with her tells you about the wind she had, not the wind you have.\nBoats on your own rung, level with you across the wind, share your wind and your waves. If one of them gains on you for a minute, something real is happening: more pressure on her side, a better angle, or you are slow. That is information you can use now."}, "B66": {"t": "Why the risk grows near the layline", "b": "On the layline you have one move left: the last tack. Every shift now costs. A header on the layline means you fall short and need two more tacks; a lift means you have overstood and sail extra distance. In the middle of the course the same shift is a chance; on the layline it is only a loss.\nAnd the layline is where the fleet gathers: a queue of boats on starboard, each in the dirty air of the one ahead, and port-tack boats arriving that must keep clear of every one of them {S1}. More boats, fewer choices, bigger mistakes."}, "B67": {"t": "Why the long tack is the safe guess", "b": "When the mark is not straight upwind, one tack points nearer to it and you have more of that tack to sail (chapter 4). Sailing it first keeps you close to the straight line to the mark and far from both laylines, so the shifts you cannot predict stay cheap.\nIt also keeps the short tack for the end, when you know more: the wind of the last few minutes, the fleet, the mark. A boat that sails the short tack first reaches the layline early, with the longest leg still to sail and no choices left on it."}, "B68": {"t": "Why a big change means a new look", "b": "Your plan was made on the wind of twenty minutes ago. A shift of ten degrees moves the favoured side; a new band of pressure on one side moves it further; a cloud can switch the sea breeze off. Each one changes the answer to the question the plan was built on.\nChecking costs ten seconds and three questions. Not checking costs the beat, because a plan kept out of habit takes you to the side that was right before the change, and the boats that looked went the other way."}, "B69": {"t": "Why clear air beats a small gain of angle", "b": "Dirty air costs about four lengths a minute (chapter 9). A small gain of angle, two or three degrees, is worth a length or two a minute, against four. A real shift is different: chapter 4's five degrees makes the lifted tack five or six lengths a minute better, and then you weigh it.\nSo a move that buys a few degrees and sails you into another boat's wind loses, and a move that keeps your air clean at a slightly worse angle usually wins."}, "B70": {"t": "Why the leaders show you the wind", "b": "The boats ahead and upwind of you are in the wind that is on its way to you. A gust they heel to now reaches you in a minute; a shift that lifts them now is the shift you will feel next. Watching them is watching the future.\nThat is also the trap. They are in the wind that is on them now, not the wind coming from beyond them, and a leader on the wrong side of a new shift is still a leader for another minute. Use them as instruments, not as a map."}, "B71": {"t": "Why a gain must be banked", "b": "A gain from a shift exists only while the leverage that made it exists. If you are 100 metres to the side of a boat and a ten degree shift puts you seven lengths ahead of her, the shift going back takes the seven lengths away again.\nCrossing her and coming back towards her takes the leverage to nearly nothing. Now the next shift moves you both the same way, and the seven lengths are yours whatever the wind does. That is banking: turning a gain that depends on the wind into one that does not."}, "B72": {"t": "Why the outside boat risks the most", "b": "Leverage is sideways distance, and the boat furthest out has the most of it on everyone. A ten degree shift that gains her seven lengths on a boat 100 metres inside her loses her seven if it goes the other way, and every boat inside her gains or loses on her at once.\nOut at a corner she also has one way to the mark (chapter 4). A header there cannot be answered with a tack towards the shift; it is a loss with no reply."}, "B73": {"t": "Why the right amount of risk depends on the race", "b": "A bet with leverage can gain seven lengths or lose seven. If you are leading, seven lengths gained changes nothing and seven lost changes everything, so the bet is bad. If you are far behind with the finish near, seven lengths lost changes little and seven gained may be the only way past, so the same bet is good.\nThe race decides how much to risk, not the wind. The wind decides which way to risk it."}, "B74": {"t": "Why a tight cover slows you", "b": "To sit on one boat's wind you must tack every time she tacks, and each tack costs a length or two. You sail where she makes you sail, not where the wind is better. And while the two of you tack together, the rest of the fleet sails the shifts and passes you both.\nA loose cover costs none of that: you stay between her and the mark, tack on your own shifts, and let the leverage stay small. She cannot pass without a shift or a gust of her own, and because you are upwind of her with little leverage between you, most of what reaches her reaches you first."}, "B75": {"t": "Why a lee-bow tack works", "b": "A boat close ahead and to leeward bends the wind off her sail onto the boat behind her, like a header (chapter 13). So a boat that tacks into that place, a length or so ahead and just to leeward, puts the other boat into bad wind at once: she points lower, slows, and has to tack away or fall back.\nIt works only if the tack is finished with room to spare. Too close and the tacking boat is still slow and head to wind when the other boat arrives; too far ahead and the bent wind misses her."}, "B76": {"t": "Why the duck is usually the cheapest move", "b": "To pass behind a boat you bear away ten or twenty degrees for a few seconds and come back up as her stern passes. You lose about a length, you keep your speed and your side, and you are on your own tack again at once.\nA tack costs a length or two by itself, and a forced tack also puts you on the tack you did not want, towards the side you did not choose. A collision or a penalty costs the race. Of the three, the duck is almost always the smallest."}, "B77": {"t": "Why tacking in front needs two clear lengths", "b": "After you pass head to wind you have to keep clear until you are close-hauled on the new tack {S5}, and a boat that has just tacked is slow for several seconds while it picks up speed again (chapter 9). If you start your tack only a length in front of a boat, you finish the tack in her path, slow, and either she has to avoid you, or she sails up under your lee bow or over the top of you, and you are slow in her bad air.\nTwo lengths of clear water give you the tack, the acceleration and the lane. Less than that, and the duck is the better move."}, "B78": {"t": "Why going the wrong way costs more than a duck", "b": "A duck costs a length. Tacking away from a boat that threatens your lane costs a length or two for the tack, and then it costs leverage: every minute you sail away from your side is a minute in which the shift you expected gains on the other side: seven or eight lengths on a ten degree shift for every hundred metres of leverage (chapter 14).\nSo when a boat makes you choose between a small loss on your side and a clean escape to the wrong side, the small loss wins."}, "B79": {"t": "Why an escape must land in clear air", "b": "Leaving a bad lane costs a tack, a length or two. If the new lane is also bad, another boat's shadow or the back of a queue, you pay again: a second tack or a minute of dirty air. Two losses for one problem.\nThe second loss is avoidable because it is visible. Before the tack, the lane you are going to is in front of you: its boats, their shadows, the water. Looking takes two seconds. Not looking costs four lengths."}, "B80": {"t": "Why a good choice can lose once", "b": "A choice is made on what you know at the time: the wind you can see, the boats, the odds. A choice that wins seven times in ten is a good choice, and it loses three times in ten. Losing once does not make it wrong, and winning once does not make a bad choice right.\nOver a series the good choices win, because the odds add up. Judge your decisions by what you knew when you made them, and the results will follow; judge them by the results and you will learn the wrong lessons from every lucky race."}, "B81": {"t": "Why a layline reached early is a dead end", "b": "On the layline you have one move left: the last tack. Every shift after that is a loss. A header means you no longer fetch the mark and need two more tacks; a lift means you have overstood and sail extra distance for nothing. In the middle of the course the same shift is a choice; on the layline it is only a bill.\nThe bill grows with the distance left. A ten degree lift with 200 metres still to sail would carry you about 35 metres, fifteen boat lengths, to windward of the mark: you bear away and reach down to it while every boat below you is lifted straight at it. A boat that tacked 100 metres later wastes half of that. Reach the layline late and the shifts have less room to hurt."}, "B82": {"t": "Why the starboard queue is slow", "b": "Boats on the starboard layline sail in a line, each in the dirty air of the one ahead (chapter 9). Every boat in the train points a little lower and goes a little slower than the clean boat at its head, so a boat that joins a hundred metres out, forty lengths back, spends a minute in bad wind before the mark and arrives behind boats it was level with.\nJoining late with a gap costs a few lengths of clean sailing below the line; joining early costs that minute. The queue is a place to pass through, not to wait in."}, "B83": {"t": "Why you come in from below the layline", "b": "A few lengths below the starboard layline you still have a choice: tack now into a gap, or sail on a little and tack into the next one. On the layline the choice is gone, and so is the room to answer a header or a boat tacking on your wind.\nFrom below you also see the train from the side, so you can pick your gap instead of being given one. The cost is that you must still make the last tack cleanly and fetch; the gain is that you decide where and when."}, "B84": {"t": "Why the port approach is a gamble", "b": "Coming into the mark on port you meet the starboard train, and every boat in it has right of way over you {S1}. At the mark the usual room rules do not help you: on a beat the mark rules do not apply between boats on opposite tacks {S6}, so being nearer the mark gives you nothing.\nYou can cross only where there is a real gap, and gaps in a train close as you watch. A duck early, and a tack into the train behind, costs two lengths; a port approach that fails costs a penalty, a collision or a stop head to wind in front of the whole fleet."}, "B85": {"t": "Why tacking in the zone is restricted", "b": "A boat that tacks from port to starboard inside the zone, near a boat that is fetching the mark and has been on starboard since she entered the zone, has just put herself in that boat's way. So the rules add a duty: she must not make that boat sail above close-hauled to avoid her, and if the starboard boat becomes overlapped inside her she must give mark-room {S6}.\nIn practice a tack inside the zone works only into a gap big enough that nobody has to alter course at all. Anything tighter is a penalty waiting to happen."}, "B86": {"t": "Why the exit is decided before the mark", "b": "In the rounding your hands are full: the sheet, the tiller, the boat heeling as you bear away, boats on both sides. Nothing new can be decided in those five seconds, so what you do after the mark is whatever you decided before it.\nThe decision is made ten to twenty lengths out: which side of the next leg, where the clear air is, what the boats ahead are doing. The sailors who round well have already left the mark in their heads before they reach it."}, "B87": {"t": "Why the rounding position is a poor judge", "b": "Your place at the mark is one number, and it mixes your choices with the wind's. A sailor who went to a corner and got a shift rounds high; a sailor who sailed a good beat and got the wrong side of a flick rounds low. The number cannot tell them apart.\nWhat you can judge are the choices: both laylines seen, the layline joined late, the approach on starboard with a gap, the rights known, the exit planned. Those five are yours every race; the number is yours only on average."}, "B88": {"t": "Why port keeps clear of starboard", "b": "Two boats on opposite tacks meet all the time on a beat, and somebody has to be the one who moves. The rules pick the port-tack boat every time two racing boats meet on a beat {S1}, except a boat going back after an early start or doing her turns, who keeps clear of everyone (chapter 12), so that nobody has to work it out in the last two seconds. The starboard boat holds her course and the port boat chooses, early, how to keep clear.\nThat is why the choice in chapter 16 is the port boat's: cross, tack or duck. The starboard boat's job is simpler and comes with a duty: hold a steady course so that the port boat can plan around it, and still avoid contact if the port boat gets it wrong."}, "B89": {"t": "Why the leeward boat has the lane", "b": "When two boats on the same tack are overlapped, the windward boat keeps clear {S2}. So the leeward boat owns the water between them: she can sail her course and, if she did not come up from behind, head up, and the windward boat has to go with her. That is the whole reason a lee-bow works (chapter 16): the boat that tacks in to leeward is the one with the rights.\nThe leeward boat's right has limits. If she changes course she must give the windward boat room to keep clear {S7}; if she came up from behind, less than two lengths to leeward of the other boat, she may not sail above her proper course while they stay overlapped and that close {S7}; and she must still avoid contact {S7}. Rights are for sailing your course, not for hitting people."}, "B90": {"t": "Why a tacking boat has no rights", "b": "From the moment you pass head to wind you are already on the new tack by the rule book's definition, but port, starboard, windward and astern are all switched off until you are close-hauled: you are turning, slow, and taking up water on both sides. So the rules make the tacking boat keep clear of everyone during the turn {S5}.\nThat is why a tack needs room (chapters 16 and 17). If another boat has to alter course while you are still turning, the tack was in the wrong place, and the penalty is yours."}, "B91": {"t": "Why right of way comes with duties", "b": "Right of way means the other boat moves, not that you may hit her. Every boat must avoid contact if she reasonably can {S7}, and a right-of-way boat that could still have turned away, and did not, has broken a rule too; she is penalised for it when the crash damages a boat or hurts someone {S7}.\nThe second duty is room. When you change course, the other boat needs time and space to keep clear of the new course; when you get right of way by your own move, a tack or a new overlap, the other boat needs a moment to respond before you can expect her to be clear {S5}{S7}. Rights are a plan the other boat can see; sudden changes take the plan away."}, "B92": {"t": "Why turns taken at once are cheap", "b": "Two turns in an Optimist take about twenty seconds and cost a few boat lengths. A protest you lose costs the race, and a collision can cost the boat. So the moment you think you may have broken a rule about another boat, the turns are the cheapest thing on the water {S7}.\nTaken at once, well clear of other boats, they also end the matter, unless you hurt someone, broke something, or gained a lot by it {S7}: nobody has to protest, and you are racing again before the fleet has moved far. Taken late, or not at all, the cost only grows."}, "B93": {"t": "Why strategy needs a fast boat", "b": "A good side of the course is worth seven lengths on a shift (chapter 3). A boat that is slow by two per cent at about three knots, one and a half metres a second, loses about two metres a minute: nearly a length. Over a ten minute beat that is about eight lengths: more than the shift was worth.\nSo every choice in this book pays only if the boat can turn it into distance. Speed is not a separate subject from strategy; it is the exchange rate."}, "B94": {"t": "Why water in the boat is slow", "b": "Water in the bottom of an Optimist is weight, and weight sits low and slides to the low side when you heel. So a wet boat is heavier to accelerate and harder to keep flat, exactly when flat matters most, in strong wind and in gusts.\nA boat full of water in the first beat costs the race; a minute on the shore prevents it."}, "B95": {"t": "Why your settings are your own", "b": "The fast setting for a sail depends on the sail's cut, the sailor's weight, the stiffness of the mast and the state of the sea. Two sailors with the same sail and different weights want different depth; the same sailor usually wants a fuller sail in chop and a flatter one in flat water.\nSo another sailor's numbers are a place to start, not an answer. The answer comes from sailing beside a partner of about your speed, changing one thing at a time, and writing down what was fast in which wind."}, "B96": {"t": "Why a leech telltale that stalls now and then means pointing", "b": "The telltale on the top batten shows what the air is doing as it leaves the back of the sail. Streaming, it says the air is flowing cleanly off the leech: the sail is open, powered, and the boat accelerates. Stalling, it says the leech is pulled in hard enough that the air tumbles behind it: more pointing, less drive.\nNeither is right all the time. One coach's baseline is flowing about half the time and hiding behind the sail the other half when the trim is right, with more flow when you need speed and more stall when you can afford to point. A leech telltale that never comes out from behind the sail is usually not pointing but over-sheeting: the whole leech is stalled and the boat is slow. Ease a little and watch your speed; in very light wind it may hang whatever you do."}, "B97": {"t": "Why you react before the heel", "b": "When a gust hits a boat that is not ready, the boat heels. A heeled hull pulls to windward, so you pull the tiller to hold the course, and a tiller pulled hard is a brake. The boat, tipped over, slides sideways more and goes forward less. So the gust that should have made you faster has made you slower, and wetter.\nReacting before the heel costs nothing: your body is already out and your hand is already on the sheet. The boat stays flat, the rudder stays straight, and all of the extra wind goes into speed."}, "B98": {"t": "Why the flat boat comes first", "b": "Every stage after the first depends on it. Hiking before the heel keeps the boat flat without easing much, so the sail keeps its power. The feather only works when the boat is fast and flat, because a slow boat turned towards the wind simply stops. And the trim back at the end only works if there is speed to keep.\nThat is why the stages are learned in order, one at a time: a flat boat, then the body early, then the tiny turn, then the exit, and only then the gust used for height. Skip a stage and the ones after it fall over."}, "B99": {"t": "Why you move in before you bear away", "b": "In a lull the wind that was holding the boat up against your weight goes away, so the boat rolls over towards you, to windward, and the sail loses its shape. Weight in at once keeps the heel where it was and the sail full; a small ease keeps the air flowing over it.\nBearing away is the last answer, not the first. A boat that is still moving coasts through a short lull on its own speed, and a big turn away costs height you have already won, and rudder drag with it, for nothing. If the lull lasts, then you sail a little lower to keep the sail working, while you look for the next pressure."}, "B100": {"t": "Why a flap beats a heel", "b": "When the boat is overpowered, something has to give: the sheet, the heel, or the course. Easing the sheet until the front of the sail flaps for a moment costs a little drive for a second. Letting the boat heel hard costs more: sideways push, rudder drag, and water coming over the side, which stays in the boat and makes every gust after it worse.\nSo in strong wind the sheet gives first and the boat stays flat and dry. Pointing high is added only when there is speed and control to spare, never instead of them."}, "B101": {"t": "Why you tap what you see, not what you hope", "b": "A plan made from hope points at the side you like; a plan made from what you can see points at the side with the wind in it. The card asks only for things you can see or test: dark water, a shift you have measured, an end the head-to-wind test gave you. If a line cannot be filled in from evidence, the honest answer is not sure, and not sure means the middle.\nWritten down, the plan also stops moving. In the last minute the mind wants to change everything; the card is what you decided while you could still think."}, "B102": {"t": "Why one first move is enough", "b": "A beat has hundreds of choices, but only one of them is made before the gun: which side first. Everything in the first minute, the lane, the hole, the first tack, serves that one line. Two first moves are no plan; a sailor with go left and maybe right on the card does neither and ends up in the middle by accident.\nOne move, and one reason to change it, the change the plan if line. With those two written down the first minute is simple, and simple is fast when seventy boats are around you."}, "B103": {"t": "Why one honest reason beats a long story", "b": "After a race the memory offers a story with many parts, and most of them are excuses. One reason forces a choice: was it the side, the shift, dirty air, or speed? The choice is what you can work on; the story is not.\nHonest matters as much as one. A bad start caused by going at the wrong end is a different lesson from one caused by being slow at the gun, and only the true one helps next week. The card is for you, so there is nobody to impress on it."}, "B104": {"t": "Why the drawing is where the learning is", "b": "A beat remembered is a feeling; a beat drawn is a shape. When you draw your track and the winner's on the same course, the difference is suddenly visible: where you went to a corner, where the shift found you on the wrong tack, where the fleet went right while you held left.\nThe pencil also asks questions the memory skips: where exactly did you tack, and why there? A sailor who draws ten beats has ten shapes to compare, and the shapes show the habit that the single race could not."}, "B105": {"t": "Why a card you can carry works", "b": "In a race there is no time to think anything new. What you can do is read: one line, a few words, that you have practised until they come out on their own. So the pocket cards hold only the words you say at each moment, in the order you say them, and nothing else.\nSaid out loud, the words also keep the order fixed: legal, fast, flat, clear, decide, in that sequence, not decide first and fast never. A card you can carry is a plan that survives seventy boats and a loud start."}, "B106": {"t": "Why one behaviour per session", "b": "A habit is built by repeating one thing until it happens without being thought about. Ten things practised at once are ten things half learned, and in a race the half-learned one is the one that fails. So each session has one chapter and one behaviour that can be seen and counted: tacks on headers, looks upwind, clear-air escapes.\nCounted matters. A behaviour you can count is one your coach can measure from the boat and one you can see improving from week to week; a behaviour you cannot count is only a feeling."}, "B107": {"t": "Why you sail every corridor before you choose", "b": "A sailor who always goes to the same side never learns what the other side does. Sailing the left, the middle and the right on purpose, one after the other on the same day, puts the three answers side by side: which corridor had the pressure, which had the better angle, which the current helped. Then the free choice is made on evidence, not on habit.\nThe guess before the free race is the test. If you can say which corridor will win and how sure you are, and you are right more often than not, you are reading the course and not just sailing it."}, "B108": {"t": "Why the coach asks instead of tells", "b": "If the coach says go left, you learn that the coach knows. If the coach asks what told you that, you learn to find the evidence yourself, and in the race the coach is outside the course and cannot speak to you.\nSo the questions after each go are always the same: what did you see, what did you expect, what happened, what will you change. Answering them is the skill; the answer itself matters less than the habit of looking for it."}, "B109": {"t": "Why five questions and not more", "b": "After a race the memory is full and most of it is noise: the wave that stopped you, the boat that hailed, the gust that nearly tipped you in. Five questions in a fixed order sort the noise: what you saw, what you planned, whether the start gave you access to the plan, what made you keep or change it, and whether you would decide the same again with what you knew.\nFive is enough to find the one moment that decided the beat, and few enough to be done on the way in. More questions find more moments, and a sailor who takes ten lessons from a race learns none of them."}, "B110": {"t": "Why you score the process and not the place", "b": "One number cannot tell you which of the five things in a finishing place moved: your decisions, your boat speed, luck (mostly the wind's), the strength of the fleet, or mistakes elsewhere in the race. A sailor who improves every decision can still finish lower in a stronger fleet, and a sailor who went to a lucky corner can finish higher while learning nothing.\nTracking how you race separates your part from the other four. Front-row starts, minutes in clear air, right shift calls, early laylines, rules risks, and whether your sureness matched what happened: each one is yours, each one you can practise, and each one moves on its own."}, "B111": {"t": "Why a good result can come from a bad decision", "b": "A decision is made with the information you have at the moment you make it. Judged afterwards with the information the race gave you later, almost any decision looks obvious, which is why hindsight teaches so little. The honest test is the fifth question: would I decide the same again with what I knew then?\nIf the answer is yes and the race went badly, the decision was not a mistake, and it stays. A bad decision is a bet with poor odds, and poor odds still come off some of the time. If the answer is no and the race went well, you were lucky and the decision changes. The place says nothing about which of the two it was; only the question does."}, "B112": {"t": "Why a small line advantage is often worth less than clear air", "b": "The gain from a slightly favoured end is fixed at the gun: a length or two, won once, and that is all. The loss from a bad start is not fixed: a boat in dirty air sails slower and lower for as long as it stays there, and a boat that has to tack away to find clean wind gives up the side it wanted. One is a length; the other is a lane for a minute, or a side for a beat.\nSo the manuals judge a start by what it gives a minute later, not by where the bow was at the gun: speed, clear air and a route to your side. A front-row start in clear air along the line beats a crowded start at the favoured end that leaves you slow with nowhere to go."}, "B113": {"t": "Why a clever move is usually not clever", "b": "Every move costs something: a tack costs a length or two before the speed is back (chapter 4), and a duck costs about a length. A move that puts you in bad air costs more, because the loss does not stop when the move is over: the boat in front keeps taking your wind, and the gap grows for as long as you stay there.\nA tactical gain is a few lengths taken from one boat. The cost of being slow and in dirty air is lengths given to every boat in the fleet. That is why the manual puts speed and clear air before any clever move, and why the test of a move is not whether it beats the boat next to you but whether you are still fast and free afterwards."}, "B114": {"t": "Why the strongest signal wins", "b": "Every clue has a size and a sureness. A gust lane you can see on the water and in the boats ahead is big and sure; a slight lift that might be a wobble is small and unsure. The value of a clue is the two together: a big sure clue is worth sailing for, a big unsure one is worth watching, and a small clue is not worth leaving your lane or your side for on its own.\nThis is why the order within the fourth step is not fixed. Pressure usually comes first because in an Optimist more wind is usually the biggest gain, and often the one you can see on the water. But a weak pressure clue loses to a sure lifted tack, and no clue that is not sure is worth leaving your lane for."}, "B115": {"t": "Why you look again", "b": "A plan is made from the wind of the moment you made it, and the wind does not stay. A shift you were sure of ten minutes ago may have turned back; the pressure you saw on the left may have come down the course and gone; the boats you were watching have moved. A plan that is not checked is a plan for a race that has already changed.\nLooking again is not the same as changing your mind. Most looks confirm the plan, and the plan stays. The look is there so that when the picture has really changed, you find out from the water and not from the boats passing you."}, "B116": {"t": "Why the race area comes before the line", "b": "Each look at the line takes a minute, and the line keeps changing until the gun, so the last look is the one that counts. The race area cannot be read in a minute. A pattern in the shifts shows itself only after several swings, a band of pressure has to be watched to see whether it stays, and the current needs a mark and a sponge. Twenty minutes is about what it takes, and nearly all of it has to be done before the warning signal, when the line and the crowd need all your attention.\nSo the order is the race area first, with a first look at your normals on the way out, then the kind of day near the line, then the line itself. A sailor who does it the other way round knows which end is favoured and nothing about where the end leads."}, "B117": {"t": "Why a partner tells you more than your own eyes", "b": "One boat can be in one place at a time. Sailing up the left and then up the right, you compare the left of ten minutes ago with the right of now, and the wind may have changed in between. Two boats sample both sides at the same minutes, so the difference between them is the difference between the sides, not between the times.\nThe partner must be about your speed, and that is the whole point: if one of you is faster the test measures the sailor, not the wind. Matched boats make the wind and the water the only thing that differs, and a boat length at the crossing is then a boat length the side gave."}, "B118": {"t": "Why the skew makes one tack long", "b": "On a square course the mark is straight upwind and the two tacks are mirror images: equal lengths, equal laylines. Move the mark to the right and the picture tilts. The port tack, which heads up and to the right, now points closer to the mark, and the starboard tack points further from it. To reach the mark you must sail more port than starboard, so port is the long tack, and its layline runs back across the course, close to the line, while the starboard layline is far out to the right.\nSo a boat that sails the short tack first meets the port layline early, with most of the beat still to sail; from the long tack both laylines stay further away."}, "B119": {"t": "Why the fleet is a clue, and a trap", "b": "Seventy boats are seventy pairs of eyes, and before the start many of them have been sailing the sides you have not had time for. Where the good sailors have gone and where they come back from tells you what they found, and a group that leans to one side has usually felt something: a shift, pressure, current.\nThe same crowd is the trap. Where the fleet goes, the air is dirty and the lanes are short, so the side the fleet has chosen is also the side where it will be hardest to sail. The clue is in what the fleet has seen; the cost is in where the fleet will be. Reading the fleet means taking the first and planning around the second."}, "B120": {"t": "Why left and right are not mirror images", "b": "After the gun every boat is on starboard, heading left. Going left means holding the tack you are already on, with nobody able to make you tack; going right means tacking onto port and crossing, or ducking, the boats still on starboard. So the left is easy to reach and the right has a price on the way out.\nAt the mark it turns round. A boat that went left comes back on port and must keep clear of every starboard boat she meets {S1}, and the mark rules give her no room against them on a beat {S6}. A boat that went right comes back on starboard with right of way. The side you choose decides which of the two prices you pay, and chapters 13 and 17 show how to pay them cheaply."}, "B121": {"t": "Why the middle loses little", "b": "A shift moves you up or down the ladder by an amount that grows with your sideways distance from the other boats (chapter 3). In the middle that distance is small on both sides, so the next shift, whichever way it goes, moves you little: you cannot win a side, but you cannot lose one either.\nThat is the trade. The middle keeps both laylines far away and both sides open, and it keeps you near the boats you are racing, whose angles show you the shifts (section 4.4). What it gives up is the big gain, which is why you leave it only for a reason you could point to."}, "B122": {"t": "Why how sure you are sets how far you go", "b": "A side is a bet, and a bet has two parts: how much you stake and how likely you are to win. The stake is leverage, the sideways distance from the fleet; the odds are how sure you are of the clue. A sure clue with a big stake is a good bet. An unsure clue with a big stake is a gamble whose size you did not choose.\nSo the rule matches the two. Sure: go, and take the leverage, because the odds are on your side. Half sure: lean that way a few lengths, a small stake on fair odds. Not sure: the middle, no stake at all. That makes risk something you decided, not something that happened to you."}, "B123": {"t": "Why the trigger is chosen before the start", "b": "In the last minute before the gun, and in the first minute after it, there is no time to think: the boats, the line and the sheet take all of it. A decision that has to be made then is made badly, or not at all, and the plan is kept out of habit after the thing it depended on has gone.\nA trigger chosen while you are calm moves the decision to a time when you could think. In the race you do not decide whether to change; you only notice whether the one thing has happened. Noticing is fast. Deciding is not."}}};
